/><slidelink slideid="_player.5xejziefxGz.5jEAcyV4kE9" displaytext="Part 2: Grade-Band Discussion Board" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xejziefxGz.6CNOanDD5RS" displaytext="Part 3: Looking Ahead at Lesson Planning" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xejziefxGz.5fBjIRFFy96" displaytext="Part 4: Assessment of Learning" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xejziefxGz.6rkKyyRTmPr" displaytext="Reflect" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5xejziefxGz.61S7VKf7TpR" displaytext="Conclusion" expand="true" /></links></slidelink></links></outline><search><slidetext slideid="6F0BRHGAiKg.5izLDpGRQGD" slidebank="false">focusing on the mathematical practices of the common core grades k-12  session 2:  a closer look at the standards for mathematical practice   session 1 review  session 2 objectives session 2  big questions </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6F0BRHGAiKg.5f92I4SrlXZ" slidebank="false">the standards for mathematical practice are eight habits of mathematical thinking that teachers expect students to put into practice across all grade levels. these mathematical practices often happen simultaneously. for example, when students make sense of a problem (practice 1) with others, they might need to justify their perspectives (practice 3).  because the common core state standards for mathematics (ccssm) are written for grades k-12, they will manifest differently at each grade level. this will become evident during the activities and discussion that take place throughout this institute.  (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010) </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6F0BRHGAiKg.5YvTXFAID1E" slidebank="false">after reading others’ responses on the discussion board, are there impacts to your instruction that you did not consider?  take a moment to reflect on this question.   #9 review your discussion board post from session 1.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6F0BRHGAiKg.5oEHCkB1WPu" slidebank="false">what are the standards for mathematical  practice, and why are they important for  mathematical proficiency? what does “understanding” in mathematics  mean, and how do the standards for  mathematical practice lead to  understanding?  1 2 learn in practice apply as you complete this session, keep the following big questions in mind:  #9 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6F0BRHGAiKg.6bGVieZSBzy" slidebank="false">briefly describe each of the standards for mathematical practice connect the standards for mathematical practice to the national council of teachers of mathematics (nctm) process standards  discuss your personal level of readiness for implementing the standards for mathematical practice at the end of this session, you will be able to </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.6pGHnueR84o" slidebank="false">embedded within the ccssm are these eight practices define how students should interact with the mathematics content at every grade level from grades k–12.  click to read more about the standards for mathematical practice. standards for  mathematical practice 8  the eight standards for mathematical practice 1. make sense of problems and persevere in solving them. 2. reason abstractly and quantitatively. 3. construct viable arguments and critique the reasoning of others. 4. model with mathematics. 5. use appropriate tools strategically. 6. attend to precision. 7. look for and make use of structure. 8. look for and express regularity in repeated reasoning. (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief  state school officers 2010, 6-8)  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.6RYZiFvZzVZ" slidebank="false">the standards for mathematical practice are connected to the nctm principles and standards. development and background of the standards for mathematical practice  nctm principles and standards balance of procedures  and understanding 1 2 3 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.6T6uwpJrog8" slidebank="false">to better understand this balanced approach, watch as envisionmath    author, dr. juanita copley, describes a balanced classroom experience.   as the nctm developed the process standards and adding it up outlined the five proficiencies, a debate ensued around the teaching of skills and the teaching of concepts.  to ensure that this debate does not continue with the ccssm, teachers must learn to teach lessons that have a balance between skills and conceptual understanding. ® #10  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.5Yy8VKMJGaV" slidebank="false">the nctm principles and standards includes the following five process standards:  1. problem solving 2. reasoning and proof 3. communication 4. representations 5. connections when developed, these process standards were intended to be the bedrock of how students would work with mathematical content. however, due to state testing requirements, the focus of teaching became about covering state documents with numerous content standards or the breadth of mathematics rather than the depth. click here to read more about the process standards. #10 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.6k7SQwTMPiw" slidebank="false">phil daro  chair, mathematics college and career readiness standards work group writing team, mathematics k-12 common core standards committee  senior fellow, pearson america’s choice  #9-10  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6hl2P7fWcp4" slidebank="false">before moving on to the next section, take a moment to explore the connections between each of the standards for mathematical practice and the nctm process standards. resources have been provided in the participant workbook to assist you in completing this activity.  note: multiple connections may  be made.  #11-12 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6Jq1w7pP1P7" slidebank="false">make sense of problems and persevere in  solving them.  (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 6) problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation drop here check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are problem solving, communication, and representation.  continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6AMJdQm0tkV" slidebank="false">reason abstractly and quantitatively.   (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 6) drop here problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are problem solving and reasoning and proof. continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6pIQSS8I7up" slidebank="false">construct viable arguments and critique the reasoning of others.  drop here problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 6) check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are reasoning and proof, communication, and representation.  continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.5scy6Qj8K7W" slidebank="false">model with mathematics.  (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 7) drop here problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are problem solving, reasoning and proof, connections, and representation. continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6imQJNtM91g" slidebank="false">use appropriate tools strategically.  (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 7) drop here problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are problem solving and representation. continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6QeJTYaK1rG" slidebank="false">attend to precision.  (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 7) drop here problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are problem solving and communication. continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6FvVzfxqNqn" slidebank="false">look for and make use of structure.  (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 8) drop here problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are problem solving, reasoning and proof, and connections. continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.5zgd0LoGzGE" slidebank="false">look for and express regularity in  repeated reasoning.  drop here problem solving nctm process standards  communication connections reasoning and proof representation (national governors association center for best practices, council of chief state school officers 2010, 8) check your answer  correct you are right! you have selected the correct response.   continue  incorrect the correct answers are problem solving, reasoning and proof, and connections. continue </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.6ld8aKlsDNL" slidebank="false">the discussion board assignment is broken into two parts: the general discussion board and the grade-band discussion board. to assist you in organizing your notes and thoughts, you can also find both parts of the assignment in the participant workbook.  the looking ahead at lesson planning assignment presents questions that assist you in transferring the learning from session 2 into your final lesson plan assignment. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.6dDpg965fID" slidebank="false">remember, as with any discussion, the communication must go two ways. as you post your responses on the discussion board, be sure to comment on your peers’ posts as well so that there is continuous conversation. once you have completed your assignment, check back often to help keep the discussion going.   part 1 general discussion board part 2 grade-band discussion board part 3 looking ahead at lesson planning part 4 assessment of learning </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.5vgUj1qdLBH" slidebank="false">what are your initial thoughts on the standards for mathematical practice? describe at least one instructional strategy or idea that balances classroom instruction between concepts and skills while incorporating the standards for mathematical practice.   1 2 access the general discussion board, and post your responses to the following two items: #13 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.5jEAcyV4kE9" slidebank="false">access your grade-band discussion board, and  post your response to the following:  pose one question that you have about incorporating the standards for mathematical practice into your everyday instruction.  #13 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.5fBjIRFFy96" slidebank="false">it is time to show what you have learned in sessions 1 and 2 of this virtual institute!  please access the assessment of learning through pearson learning studio.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.6CNOanDD5RS" slidebank="false">in the past, how have you addressed the five nctm process standards? as you think about planning your future mathematics lessons, consider the following question: #39 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.6rkKyyRTmPr" slidebank="false">reflect on this session. what are the standards for mathematical practice, and why are they important for mathematical proficiency? what does “understanding” in mathematics mean, and how do the standards for mathematical practice lead to understanding?  #9 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.61S7VKf7TpR" slidebank="false">congratulations on completing session 2!  </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="&lt;p align='left'&gt;&lt;font face='Arial' size='11' color='#444444'&gt;Here are some useful links and documents:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>